From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v8-embed-feedstock <noreply@github.com>
Date: Tue, 2 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] torque: find a source whatever separator it was named with

SourceFileMap stores each .tq source under the path it was given on the
command line and looks sources up by exact string compare.  Torque
itself performs one such lookup by a fixed name: ClassType::
AttributedToFile files every class without a hand-written header under
"src/objects/torque-defined-classes.tq".

gyp's ninja generator on Windows passes the sources as
src\objects\torque-defined-classes.tq.  The lookup fails, silently: the
classes are attributed to an invalid source, their definitions are
written nowhere, torque-defined-classes-tq.inc comes out empty (the
source itself defines nothing), instance-types.h still lists them, and
the first translation unit to include objects-body-descriptors-inl.h
fails with "incomplete type 'StrongDescriptorArray' named in nested
name specifier".  MSBuild spells the arguments with slashes, which is
why node's own Windows build never sees it.

Both sides now normalise to forward slashes, so a source is found under
either spelling.  The stored path is also what names the outputs, so
they come out with slashes regardless, as they do on every other
platform.
---
 deps/v8/src/torque/source-positions.cc | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/deps/v8/src/torque/source-positions.cc b/deps/v8/src/torque/source-positions.cc
--- a/deps/v8/src/torque/source-positions.cc
+++ b/deps/v8/src/torque/source-positions.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 #include "src/torque/source-positions.h"
 
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <fstream>
 #include "src/torque/utils.h"
 
@@ -37,16 +38,31 @@
   return path_from_root;
 }
 
+namespace {
+
+// Sources are looked up by the path they were named by, so a path has one
+// spelling here whatever separator the build system used: torque itself
+// asks for "src/objects/torque-defined-classes.tq" when it files the
+// classes that have no header of their own, and a build that passed the
+// same file as src\objects\torque-defined-classes.tq must find it.
+std::string WithForwardSlashes(std::string path) {
+  std::replace(path.begin(), path.end(), '\\', '/');
+  return path;
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
 // static
 SourceId SourceFileMap::AddSource(std::string path) {
-  Get().sources_.push_back(std::move(path));
+  Get().sources_.push_back(WithForwardSlashes(std::move(path)));
   return SourceId(static_cast<int>(Get().sources_.size()) - 1);
 }
 
 // static
 SourceId SourceFileMap::GetSourceId(const std::string& path) {
+  const std::string wanted = WithForwardSlashes(path);
   for (size_t i = 0; i < Get().sources_.size(); ++i) {
-    if (Get().sources_[i] == path) {
+    if (Get().sources_[i] == wanted) {
       return SourceId(static_cast<int>(i));
     }
   }
